Single branch: if condition, action, end. Two branches: if condition, action1, else, action2, end. Example:
a = 100;
if a < 20
 fprintf('a is less than 20\n');
else
 fprintf('a is not less than 20\n');
end
fprintf('value of a is : %d\n',a);

age = input("Enter a age ");
if age > 0 && age <=13
 fprintf('young \n ');
elseif age > 13 && age <20
 fprintf('teen \n ');
elseif age > 20 && age <60
 fprintf('adult \n ');
elseif age <=100
 fprintf('senior \n ');
else
 fprintf('sorry \n ');
end
fprintf("Thanks, keep safe");
These are source syntax, not recommended age rules: age 20 and nonpositive ages can fall to senior; age 60 already reaches senior correctly.

grade = 'B';
switch(grade)
case 'A'
 fprintf('Excellent!\n');
case 'B'
 fprintf('Well done\n');
case 'C'
 fprintf('Well done\n');
case 'D'
 fprintf('You passed\n');
case 'F'
 fprintf('Better try again\n');
otherwise
 fprintf('Invalid grade\n');
end

x = 3, y = 3;
switch x
case x*(x==y)
 disp('x and y are equal');
case x*(x>y)
 disp('x is greater than y');
otherwise
 disp('x is less than y');
end
For these fixed values the first case matches. This numeric-case trick is not a robust general relational comparison: x=0 can match a false case multiplied by zero; ordinary if/elseif is clearer for ordering.

